			<div class="content" id="post-content-4652726">
									

<p>The earliest version of this article looked radically, radically different. It was initially inspired by the case of &quot;Herman Emmanuel Fankem&quot;, a Canadian prisoner who is not actually named that. His real name and identity is unknown. The idea of a prisoner without an identity or reason to be in prison fascinated me, and I started a draft along those lines.</p>
<p>Some time later, I learned of the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Man_in_the_Iron_Mask">Man in the the Iron Mask</a>, a French prisoner from the 1600s with both unknown identity and crime. This fascinated me, and I started working from there again, scrapping everything I had before.</p>
<p>I've written a few mystery SCPs in the past without a clear explanation or authorial answer, and I wanted to avoid that this time. I thought of an anomaly with a good reason for the Foundation to be unaware, and went from there, as well as putting a few bureaucratic reasons in (I've been a fan of the Foundation inheriting anomalies from precursors and not fully understanding them for a while).</p>
<p>The second draft of this looked rather different. It had the same ConProcs and Description, and two addenda &#8212; the Estate <em>noir</em> letter and the Interview. But it didn't have any mention of Dr. Cocteau, or his story.</p>
<p>MaliceAforethought looked at it, and told me that while the story was put together in a new way, there wasn't really anything there he hadn't seen before. It was a collection of familiar tropes with a new coat of paint. I was already feeling that the draft was missing something, and wasn't fond of it. Back to the drawing board.</p>
<p>Enter Dr. Clouzot. As I was thinking, I came up with the idea for a Foundation researcher doing the investigation into SCP-5041 and in the process killing it. The cause of death went through a few variations, but settled on the diabetes: firmly Clouzot's fault, truly accidental and oh so mundane.</p>

