<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Response</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Thank you for reading this, I'm glad you enjoyed the story. I've made a few revisions to this typoes you pointed out.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Is the &quot;SCC&quot; abbreviation used anywhere else? I've never seen it before and it kinda stands out as unusual.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Nope, made it up here. It's just an acronym, so I don't get what you mean when you say it feels unusual.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The tone of this seems a bit iffy. What are the procedures if someone does do this? Even if its reason isn't known to staff, I doubt they would let it remain so imprecise.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Added a line about amnestic treatment if that's violated.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>What sort of entertainment media doesn't have any characters? It seems fairly restrictive; even music without lyrics can &quot;refer to&quot; an individual by its history. Perhaps stating here what entertainment media is allowed would be more appropriate. The Foundation errs on the side of caution with these things.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The refer to is a bit restrictive, I've changed that to depictions only. Examples of appropriate media would be poetry that doesn't have a speaker/subject, simple video games like Pong and Tetris, nature documentaries with the narration removed, instrumental music, or artistic materials. This is a very broad category, and it's really up to the containment staff to decide what's appropriate. Putting firm guidelines down is too restrictive.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The prisoner is to be allowed the materials with which to write and paint.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd consider these to be entertainment materials.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This needs elaboration or redaction, especially the bit about &quot;otherwise cease to exist&quot;.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Looks like a quote got skipped, but there is a psuedo-redaction here, in that the Foundation doesn't have a full understanding of how that process occurs. They know a village is wiped out from existence, but that happened two hundred years before they even existed. The Foundation can't elaborate any further.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>There's no link back to the original page once you're on the updated one.</p>
<p>Why does the main link take me to an out-of-date entry?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>These are mostly conceits of listpages as a narrative device. For what it's worth, the first isn't important (and your back button exists). For the second, the intended logic is that you are reading this document as it updates. That's not particularly evident, but that isn't a unique problem to this article, and dozens of listpage articles have the same problem. It's not something I'm going to fix.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>How did he kill a whole town with these powers, yet a single guy having diabetes killed him?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Most people in 1713 weren't walking around with illnesses that could result in them dying in a few hours/days without treatment. That's something modern medicine allows for that wouldn't have happened in the past. The people in the village were healthy enough to live for another week or so &#8212; but 5041 kills them first, and any ailments they might have had fade away. There's also the fact that it could have gotten medical treatment in the past, which isn't the case in total isolation in a cell.</p>
</div>
