			<div class="content" id="post-content-4683121">
									

<p>Well, see, the thing is that researching the anomaly was not actually his job. Clouzot's job as the HMCL is to supervise containment and make sure that is carried out well. Research is only necessary as a means to refine the Containment Procedures, which he notes have been in place for a while and are unlikely to be amended. Doctor Lebeau was on the job for decades and never felt it necessary to do all this research and form a theory.</p>
<p>Further, his actions are in violation of the spirit, if not the exact letter of the Containment Procedures. While there's nothing that prevented him, he should have picked up on this sooner. Further, a review of his logs &#8212; which would be conducted just to see if there was enough for an indictment &#8212; would indicate he wasn't doing this for the benefit of containment, but because he was angling for a promotion, going so far as to ignore the anomaly when it begged him to stop. That's the malicious intent right there, a reckless disregard for the person's concerns. He brushes them away immediately without really considering why he was asked to stop.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Considering the nature of the Foundation's work, an anomaly dying because of complete lack of knowledge, and no malicious intent or mala praxis, is one of those things that would happen eventually.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I mean yeah, the charge against him at the end is manslaughter, not murder. There's a legal distinction between the two and the charge was chosen intentionally. Manslaughter doesn't require malicious intent. They're bringing him up on charges of negligent homicide.</p>

